<div style="flex:1; padding-left:14px;">
<h1 style="margin-top:0;">
      <strong>Filtering and Transparency</strong>
    </h1>

<p style="font-size:14pt; margin:0 0 10px 0;">
      When you scroll through a feed (videos, posts, news, shopping), you’re not seeing everything available.
      You’re seeing what an algorithm decided to show you based on <strong>filters</strong>.
 </p>

<p style="font-size:14pt; margin:0 0 16px 0;">
      Sometimes those filters are helpful. Sometimes they can hide information in ways you don’t notice.
    </p>
 </div>
 
 </div>
 
<hr style="border:none; border-top:1px solid #e0e0e0; margin:16px 0;">

<h2>
      <strong>Scenario: The Feed You See</strong>
    </h2>
<p style="font-size:14pt; margin:0 0 10px 0;">
      Imagine two teachers, <strong>Ms. Rivera</strong> and <strong>Mr. Chen</strong>, open the same app during lunch
      to look for classroom ideas about AI.
    </p>

<p style="font-size:14pt; margin:0;">
      They type the same search — <em>“AI projects for beginners”</em> — but their feeds look very different.
    </p>

 



<div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; margin-top:10px;"> <div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"> <div style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Feed A (Ms. Rivera)</div> <div style="font-size:13pt; color:#555; margin-bottom:10px;">Mostly upbeat “AI makes teaching easier” content</div>

<div style="background:#f7f7f7; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px;">
